      { q: "What is a good ROI for automation?", a: "A well-scoped automation project should deliver first-year ROI of 150 to 300 percent, with subsequent years improving as implementation costs are not repeated. Projects below 100 percent ROI in the first year may still be justified if they address compliance, scalability, or strategic needs beyond direct cost savings." },
      { q: "How long does it take for automation to pay for itself?", a: "Most properly scoped business automations achieve payback within 60 to 120 days. According to Deloitte, 68 percent of businesses see payback within 12 months. Projects with payback periods exceeding 18 months should be reevaluated for scope appropriateness." },
      { q: "Should I include soft benefits in my ROI calculation?", a: "Base your primary ROI calculation on direct, measurable benefits: time savings, error reduction, and revenue impact. Present indirect benefits like employee satisfaction, scalability, and compliance improvement as additional upside, but do not let them carry the business case alone." },
      { q: "What is the typical cost of a business automation project?", a: "Simple integrations cost $1,000 to $5,000. Mid-complexity automations with AI components range from $5,000 to $25,000. Ongoing costs for platforms and maintenance typically run $200 to $500 per month. Enterprise-scale projects exceed these ranges significantly." },
      { q: "How do I account for employee time savings if I do not reduce headcount?", a: "Time savings convert to real value when freed hours are redirected to revenue-generating or value-adding work. Calculate the dollar value of the redirected time based on the fully loaded hourly cost. If freed time is not redirected productively, the savings are theoretical rather than actual." }
